        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Jōmon culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Japanese archipelago from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, with most people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> and Kyushu; the Jōmon were mostly absent from Okinawa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>. Key sites: Odai Yamamoto I, Kamikuroiwa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> cave and the Ikawazu shell mound at Tahara.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 14,000–300 BCE: Incipient 13,750–8,500 BCE, Initial 8,500–5,000, Early 5,000–3,520, Middle 3,520–2,470, Late 2,470–1,250 and Final 1,250–500 BCE, the end of the Final phase now refined to c. 300 BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">cord-marked pottery among the oldest in the world; “flame style” vessels; dogū figurines; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a>; magatama curved stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>; pit-houses; shell middens; tended groves of chestnut and lacquer trees; the early domestication of the adzuki bean</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated sites and shell middens; pottery typology (some 70 styles) and radiocarbon dating; skeletal and dental studies; ancient DNA and full-genome analyses; loanwords in Japanese. The Jōmon left no writing.</span></div>
        </div>
