    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>For some 14,000 years the people of the Japanese archipelago lived mainly by hunting, gathering and fishing, yet many settled in villages, reaching a considerable degree of sedentism and cultural complexity.</li><li>Jōmon potters made the world’s oldest known ceramics, around 14,500 BCE; their pottery was typically decorated by pressing cords into the wet clay, the pattern that gives the culture its name.</li><li>The Jōmon people later mixed with the Yayoi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers: modern Japanese carry part of their ancestry, and two Jōmon populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> merged to form the proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A domesticated peach, already similar to modern cultivated forms, appears at Jōmon sites around 4700–4400 BCE, earlier than a variety so close to modern peaches is attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> itself.</p></aside>
    </section>

